//! std.http — an HTTP client, plus the `Request`/`Response`/`HttpError` types the
//! server shares.
//!
//! Import as: import std.http
//!
//! ```thera
//! let resp = http.get('https://example.com/api/thing')?;
//! if resp.is_ok() {
//!     let doc = resp.json()?;
//! }
//!
//! let made = http.post('https://example.com/things', body: payload.bytes(),
//!     headers: ['content-type': 'application/json'])?;
//! ```
//!
//! **`http://` and `https://`.** An `https` URL verifies the server's certificate
//! chain and host name against the bundled roots; a certificate that doesn't check
//! out is an `HttpError.Tls` — its own variant because, unlike a `Connect`,
//! retrying will not fix it. A 4xx/5xx is a `Response`, not an error: `HttpError`
//! is for exchanges that did not happen. Redirects are not followed, and there is
//! no connection pooling (one connection per request).
//!
//! `get`/`post`/`send` buffer the body, which is what almost every caller wants.
//! For one that should be consumed as it arrives — a `text/event-stream`, a large
//! download — `stream` returns once the head is in, with the body as an
//! `io.Reader`; `with_stream` is the same thing with the close taken care of:
//!
//! ```thera
//! http.with_stream(request, handle: (s) => {
//!     for ev in sse.events(s.body) { … }   // std.http.sse
//!     return Result.Ok(void);
//! })?;
//! ```
//!
//! This is a barrel: it re-exports its sibling files (see docs/language.md) and
//! defines nothing of its own — it re-exports
//! [std.http.common](common/common.thera) (the shared codec and types) and the
//! `client` sibling, so `http.get(url)` stays the spelling of the most-written line
//! here.
//!
//! **The layout, and why.** `std.http.server` and `std.http.sse` are separate
//! libraries — separate *directories*, since from outside a directory library the
//! only importable surface is its barrel. That is what forces `common` to be a
//! directory too: the server cannot reach a private sibling of `std.http`, so the
//! code they share has to be a library of its own. The payoff is that serving never
//! pulls in the client, and that `sse` (which needs nothing but `io.Reader`)
//! doesn't put `Event` and `Decoder` into the namespace whose main line is
//! `http.get(url)`. The client stays a private sibling *here* because the client is
//! what `std.http` is for.
//!
//! So: `import std.http` for the client and the types, `import std.http.server` to
//! serve, `import std.http.sse` to decode an event stream. See docs/stdlib.md §
//! `std.http`.

pub import 'common'; // Request, Response, HttpError, status codes, the HTTP/1.1 codec
pub import 'client'; // get / post / send
